# AGRDA — Audit-Gated Randomized Dose Attribution of Memory-Subsystem Interference Among Co-located Containers

> **Research Prototype — B.Tech CSE Project**
> **Status: Simulation / Demo Mode Only**
> ⚠️ All results from this software are labelled **SIMULATION – NOT A MEASURED RESULT** until real Linux hardware experiments are conducted.

---

## What Is AGRDA?

AGRDA tests whether an **audit-gated randomized intervention** method can reduce false memory-specific attribution of performance interference between co-located containers.

It keeps three quantities **strictly separate**:

| Quantity | What it is | How it is estimated |
|---|---|---|
| **Intervention effect** | Change in victim performance caused by randomly throttling a suspect | Randomized dose–response regression |
| **Memory-mediated effect** | Portion attributable to the memory subsystem | Conditional interpretation via 9-check audit |
| **Total contribution** | Suspect's contribution to existing degradation | Requires a justified extrapolation model |

**Passing the audit means only *supported* memory-specific attribution — not proof that the exclusion restriction is true.**

---

## Platform Status

| Feature | This host | Required for real experiments |
|---|---|---|
| OS | Windows 10 | Bare-metal Linux |
| cgroup v2 | ❌ | ✅ Required |
| Intel RDT / MBA | ❌ (i3-7020U) | ✅ Xeon/server CPU |
| perf_event counters | ❌ | ✅ |
| Docker / Podman | Detected at runtime | ✅ |
| **Capability Level** | **SIMULATION_ONLY** | FULL_REAL |

